70: 
71: ### 2.8 Model development
72: Algorithms: penalized logistic regression (elastic net), random forest, and gradient boosting (LightGBM). Development proceeded in two selection steps, both confined to the KCHS training set. First, the gradient-boosting hyperparameters and the feature representation were chosen by a randomized search over 24 LightGBM configurations crossed with two representations?main effects only, versus main effects plus four candidate interaction terms, specified when the search was written but not named in any earlier document (age▼self-rated health, self-rated health▼oral health, age▼employment, income▼employment)?evaluated by 4-fold survey-weighted cross-validation in a 300,000-record subsample of the training set; the main-effects-only representation won, and adding interaction terms did not improve cross-validated discrimination. Second, all three algorithms were tuned over comparable 5-configuration grids under an identical protocol (5-fold stratified cross-validation in a 200,000-record subsample of the training set, scored by unweighted cross-validated AUROC) and compared at each algorithm's best configuration, so that no algorithm was advantaged by differential tuning effort. The analysis plan fixed the primary-model rule in advance: highest cross-validated AUROC, with differences smaller than 0.005 treated as ties and broken by proximity of the calibration slope to 1, and only then by parsimony. The pipeline as executed ranked the algorithms directly on AUROC and then calibration slope without constructing the tie band, so the plan's rule was checked retrospectively. **No NHANES data were used in any selection or tuning step.** Complex-survey weights were handled by unweighted training with weighted evaluation (probed in sensitivity). LightGBM (0.840) and random forest (0.835) differ by 0.0046 and therefore fall inside the tie band, so the plan's tie-break applies; it selects LightGBM, whose cross-validated calibration slope was 1.003 against 1.203 for random forest. Elastic net (0.822) lies outside the band and the parsimony criterion is not reached, so the retrospective check reproduces the model the pipeline selected (Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S11). LightGBM was refitted on the full 500,000-record training set; the selected hyperparameters and the full search grids are reported in the model card, which is deposited with the code (see Data Availability). The selected row-subsampling fraction is inactive in the deployed configuration because the subsampling frequency was left at its default of zero, so the fitted model uses all rows at every boosting iteration.
73: 
74: ### 2.9 Performance evaluation
75: Discrimination (AUROC, AUPRC), calibration, and operating characteristics, all complex-survey?weighted. Calibration was summarized by the calibration slope and by calibration-in-the-large (CITL), the latter estimated as the intercept of a weighted logistic model with the linear predictor entered as an offset (ie, with the slope fixed at 1), together with the Brier score and expected calibration error (ECE); a positive CITL indicates that observed risk exceeds predicted risk. Ninety-five percent CIs are reported for AUROC and AUPRC only; calibration measures and operating characteristics are point estimates. CIs were obtained by a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap with 1000 replicates, for cohort-level and subgroup estimates alike: within each design stratum, *n*<sub>h</sub>?1 primary sampling units were resampled with replacement and the survey weights rescaled by *n*<sub>h</sub>/(*n*<sub>h</sub>?1) times the selection count [16]. Rescaling is necessary because most NHANES strata contain only two primary sampling units, for which an ordinary within-stratum cluster bootstrap understates variance. We verified the implementation against a closed-form design-based (linearized ultimate-cluster) standard error for weighted depression prevalence: bootstrap 0.00466 vs design 0.00471 in the pre-pandemic cycle and 0.00802 vs 0.00796 in the post-pandemic cycle, i.e. agreement within 1%. For KCHS the stratum is defined as survey year crossed with the design stratum, because weights are normalized within year and stratum codes recur across years. Subgroup intervals are domain estimates: replicate weights are generated once on the full cohort design and then applied to each subgroup, rather than rebuilding the design after subsetting. Strata left with a single primary sampling unit are pooled into one pseudo-stratum so their variance contribution is not lost. Clinical utility by decision-curve analysis [8].
76: 
77: ### 2.10 External validation strategy
78: Geographic: KCHS-trained model applied to NHANES without refitting. Temporal/COVID: evaluated separately in pre and post cycles. Recalibration: intercept/slope recalibration applied out-of-sample (cross-fit) and recovery quantified?updating calibration only, without re-estimating predictor effects [6,7].
79: 
80: ### 2.11 Interpretability, fairness, deployment
81: SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form. Both the predictor order and the item count were determined entirely inside the development training set, which was split into a 450,000-record fitting set and a 50,000-record selection set: predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance computed on a random 20,000-record subsample of the fitting set, refitted at every item count from 1 to 17, and the item count was chosen on selection-set AUROC. The item count follows the analysis plan's margin ? the smallest set within 0.02 AUROC of the full model, subject to a 5?10 item ceiling; a variant using a stricter 0.01 margin is reported alongside it and flagged as post hoc (＝3.6). The internal hold-out and both NHANES cohorts were used only for evaluation (＝3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.
82: 
83: ### 2.12 Sensitivity analyses
84: Pre-specified and additional analyses (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7): outcome cut-off (￣5 as well as ￣10, evaluated internally and in both external cohorts), removal of the three added predictors (⊥14), removal of food insecurity only (⊥16), weighting approach (weighted vs unweighted training), alternative imputation (missing indicators for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of continuous predictors), an age lower bound of 30 years applied to the training *and* evaluation samples, and?as pre-specified in the protocol?a continuous-score analysis evaluating how the model ranks the untransformed PHQ-9 total, and alternative definitions of the alcohol predictor (drinking at least twice weekly, binge drinking, or all three indicators together in place of current drinking). The self-report (KCHS) versus measured (NHANES) difference in BMI is inherent to the data sources and is addressed under harmonization cautions (＝2.5) and Limitations rather than by swapping measurements. All analyses were performed in Python 3.14 (scikit-learn 1.9.0, lightgbm 4.6.0, shap 0.52.0, numpy 2.4.5, pandas 3.0.3, scipy 1.18.0, pyreadstat 1.3.5, matplotlib 3.10.9); complex-survey weighting, the stratified cluster bootstrap, calibration measures, and decision-curve net benefit were implemented directly rather than through a survey-analysis package, and the implementations are released with the code.
85: 
86: ### 2.13 Data-integrity audit
87: An adversarial audit corrected three silent data-processing defects before final analysis: (1) living-alone fabricated to a constant in NHANES pre (DMDHHSIZ absent ⊥ constant imputation) ? corrected to preserve missingness; (2) NHANES post physical-activity coded 0 for all respondents due to string-typed unit fields mis-parsed to NaN ? corrected with explicit unit mapping; (3) an income refusal code (77777) not treated as missing, placing non-respondents in the top income quintile ? corrected. These affected data integrity but not the substantive conclusions.
88: 
89: ### 2.14 Ethical Considerations
90: KCHS and NHANES are public, de-identified secondary datasets. NHANES data collection was approved by the NCHS Research Ethics Review Board, and KCHS is conducted under the authority of the KDCA. The present secondary analysis was reviewed by the Institutional Review Board of Daejeon University, which granted an exemption from review (approval number [to be inserted]) on the grounds that the study uses publicly available and de-identified data, collects and records no personal identifiers, and poses no more than minimal risk (no participant contact, intervention, or biological sampling). No re-identification was attempted, no compensation was involved, and no individually identifiable information is reported.
91: 
92: ---
93: 
94: ## 3. Results
95: 
96: ### 3.1 Participant characteristics
97: Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ￣10): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates?e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 3.7% in KCHS vs 16.4% and 17.2% in the NHANES cycles, computed among respondents with the item recorded (missing 22.1%, 6.0% and 6.3%, respectively; every characteristic in Table S6 uses available-case denominators)?consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ￣20 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210 (item-level detail in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S10). Non-response was differential?higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents?so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed the observed prevalence by ¯0.1 percentage points in both cycles (unweighted: 9.23%⊥9.27% pre, 12.69%⊥12.78% post). Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.
98: 
99: ### 3.2 Model development and internal performance
100: LightGBM was the primary model selected a priori by internal cross-validated AUROC (＝2.8). Internal hold-out AUROC was **0.832 (95% CI 0.823?0.841)**, AUPRC 0.233, Brier 0.031, calibration slope 1.00, CITL +0.10 ? **H1 met**.
101: 
102: ### 3.3 Geographic and temporal (COVID) external validation
103: See Table 2 and Figures 2 and 3. Discrimination remained clinically useful but lower: pre **0.772 (0.735?0.808)**, post **0.769 (0.739?0.799)**. Calibration deteriorated in both directions of the calibration line: the slope fell to 0.66 (pre) and 0.68 (post), and CITL rose to +0.49 and +1.11, indicating that the Korea-trained model systematically *under*-predicted absolute risk in the US?as expected from the large prevalence difference (KCHS 3.6% vs NHANES 8.4% and 12.2%). Out-of-sample intercept/slope recalibration restored calibration (slope⊥0.99, CITL⊥0.00) with discrimination essentially unchanged ? **H2 and H3 supported**.
104: 
105: **Table 2. Discrimination and calibration (primary model, LightGBM, 17 predictors, weighted).**
106: 
107: | Metric | Internal | NHANES pre | pre (recal.) | NHANES post | post (recal.) |
108: |---|---|---|---|---|---|
109: | AUROC | 0.832 | 0.772 | 0.769 | 0.769 | 0.767 |
110: | 95% CI | 0.823?0.841 | 0.735?0.808 | ? | 0.739?0.799 | ? |
111: | AUPRC | 0.233 | 0.267 | 0.262 | 0.346 | 0.346 |
112: | 95% CI | 0.216?0.254 | 0.211?0.332 | ? | 0.307?0.390 | ? |
113: | Brier | 0.031 | 0.071 | 0.070 | 0.099 | 0.093 |
114: | ECE | 0.004 | 0.032 | ? | 0.067 | ? |
115: | Calibration slope | 1.00 | 0.66 | 0.99 | 0.68 | 0.99 |
116: | CITL | +0.10 | +0.49 | 0.00 | +1.11 | 0.00 |
117: 
118: CIs are from a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap (1000 replicates; ＝2.9). CITL is the offset-model calibration intercept, so positive values denote under-prediction of observed risk. Expected calibration error (ECE) confirms excellent internal calibration (0.004) with drift on external application (0.032 pre, 0.067 post), consistent with the calibration pattern and recoverable by recalibration.
119: 
120: Recalibration is intercept/slope updating applied out-of-sample (5-fold cross-fit); because each fold uses a separate monotone calibration map, the recalibrated AUROC may differ trivially (by ¯0.003) from the original and is not the target of recalibration (calibration slope/CITL are). Internal calibration uses the original probabilities. Race/ethnicity is used only for the NHANES fairness analysis, not as a predictor.
121: 
122: ### 3.4 Effect of the added predictors
123: To avoid advantaging the larger predictor set through tuning, the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search under the identical internal protocol (＝2.8; best internal CV-AUROC 0.824). The three added predictors improved external discrimination over this independently tuned 14-predictor model: pre 0.749⊥**0.772** (汁+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; *P*<.001), post 0.753⊥**0.769** (汁+0.016, 95% CI +0.008 to +0.023; *P*<.001); *P* values are from a paired rescaled cluster bootstrap with replicates centred on the null and a Monte-Carlo correction (2000 replicates), with internal AUROC rising 0.822⊥0.832. Independent tuning did not help the 14-predictor model?running it with the 17-predictor configuration gave nearly identical results (pre 0.750, post 0.754)?so the gain reflects predictor content rather than modeling strategy. Removing food insecurity alone (16-predictor model) retained most of the gain (pre 0.767, post 0.760), confirming the improvement does not depend on the single most weakly harmonized predictor.
124: 
125: ### 3.5 Why external discrimination plateaus at ~0.77: concept shift
126: Extensive leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments: feature engineering, hyperparameter search, heterogeneous ensembling, transfer-robust internal selection, domain adaptation, alternative losses, and structural variants) did not exceed external AUROC ~0.77 when model selection was restricted to KCHS-internal criteria. Apparent gains from externally-tuned variants (e.g., specific interaction terms, ensemble weights) did not survive leakage control.
127: 
128: We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate ＝2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram-gradient-boosting and LightGBM learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only 0.765?0.772 (best learner 0.772, 95% CI 0.749?0.795 by the same rescaled cluster bootstrap). The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **matches the best in-domain performance any of these learners achieved when fitted directly to NHANES**. This is an empirical benchmark over the learners we tried, not a proof of a maximum; it is estimated on the pooled pre+post sample whereas the transported figures are cycle-specific, and its interval overlaps the transported estimates, so the comparison is approximate and cannot exclude a modest true gap. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81?0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raise?and slightly lowered?external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding the remaining harmonized-but-unused survey items did not help: public-assistance receipt alone left external AUROC unchanged (0.772/0.769), and adding all nine remaining candidates together slightly *reduced* it (?0.003 pre, ?0.001 post in the exploration run). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. Sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance and omitted harmonized information were each tested and none accounts for the gap. The most consistent remaining explanation is **shift in the predictor?outcome relationship itself**?concept shift?although the surveys also differ in how several predictors are measured (＝2.5) and the external cohorts are complete-case samples with differential depression-module non-response (＝3.1), so measurement and selection effects cannot be separated from concept shift with these data. What the evidence does establish is that the gap is not closable by recalibration, which corrects calibration rather than rank ordering, nor by more data or a different learner.
129: 
130: ### 3.6 Parsimonious deployment model
131: Predictor order and item count were both fixed inside the training set (＝2.11). On the 50,000-record selection set, discrimination rose steeply to five items and then flattened: 1 item 0.753; 3 items 0.790; **5 items 0.820**; 6 items 0.822; 7 items 0.829; 8 items 0.832; 12 items 0.834; 17 items 0.835.
132: 
133: Applying the margin recommended in the analysis plan?the smallest set staying within 0.02 AUROC of the full model, subject to a 5?10 item ceiling?selects **five items**: self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration and self-rated oral health. Evaluated afterwards on data that played no part in selection, this model achieved hold-out AUROC **0.809**, NHANES pre **0.742** and NHANES post **0.746**, against 0.832/0.773/0.769 for the full 17-predictor model fitted on the same data ? a loss of 0.023 internally, 0.031 pre-pandemic and 0.023 post-pandemic. Because the protocol described the tolerable loss qualitatively rather than numerically, whether this satisfies **H4** is a judgment: the model meets the plan's selection margin internally, but the external loss is larger than the internal one, so we regard H4 as only partially supported.
134: 
135: A stricter 0.01 margin would instead select seven items, adding employment and partnership, with hold-out 0.819 and external 0.760 (pre) and 0.758 (post) ? losses of 0.013, 0.012 and 0.010. We report this variant because it transports better, but that comparison inspected the NHANES cohorts and is therefore post hoc: its external figures are exploratory rather than an unbiased estimate for a prospectively chosen seven-item form. The same caution applies to the observation that an eighth item, smoking, raises pre-pandemic discrimination to 0.770 while lowering post-pandemic discrimination to 0.753. Choosing between the five- and seven-item forms should be settled in new validation data. The full k=1?17 trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
136: 
137: ### 3.7 Sensitivity analyses
138: Discrimination was stable across specifications that alter modeling choices rather than the target condition (Figure 4): weighted training (0.831/0.773/0.764 internal/pre/post), missing-indicator imputation (0.833/0.770/0.767), single chained-equations imputation of the continuous predictors (0.833/0.770/0.767), and the 16- and 14-predictor variants (＝3.4) all lay within 0.01 of the primary model internally. Restricting both training and evaluation to respondents aged ￣30 years *improved* discrimination (0.840/0.784/0.788), consistent with the lower subgroup performance observed at ages 20?39 (＝3.8). Changing the outcome definition to PHQ-9 ￣5 lowered discrimination throughout (0.759/0.736/0.739) at a much higher weighted prevalence (16.1%/25.0%/32.1%), as expected when the target shifts from moderate-or-worse symptoms to any symptom burden; notably, the Korea?US gap narrows from about 0.06 AUROC at the ￣10 threshold to about 0.02 at ￣5 (0.759 vs 0.736?0.739), suggesting that the transportability loss is concentrated at the more severe end of the symptom distribution. Replacing current drinking with drinking at least twice weekly, with binge drinking, or entering all three indicators together ? the alternative alcohol definitions listed in the analysis plan ? changed discrimination by at most 0.003 externally (pre 0.769?0.772, post 0.769?0.770). In the pre-specified continuous-score analysis, the deployed classifier ranked the untransformed PHQ-9 total with weighted Spearman 伏=0.378 internally, 0.403 pre-pandemic and 0.439 post-pandemic, and a model trained directly on the continuous score performed similarly (伏=0.402/0.403/0.424; weighted R昌 0.216/0.166/0.102), so the transportability pattern is not an artifact of dichotomizing the outcome. Data-integrity fixes (＝2.13) did not alter substantive conclusions. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7.
139: 
140: ### 3.8 Interpretability, fairness, and clinical utility
141: Global SHAP importance, computed in the development fitting partition, was dominated by self-rated health (mean |SHAP| 0.57), followed by age (0.38), sex (0.29), sleep duration (0.26), **self-rated oral health (0.23)**, **employment (0.17)**, and partnership (0.16); food insecurity contributed modestly (0.06, ranked 10th). Two of the three added predictors (oral health, employment) thus rank within the top six, consistent with their measured incremental value (Figure 5). The rank ordering of predictor importance was highly concordant between KCHS and both NHANES cycles (Spearman 伏=0.97 pre, 0.99 post), indicating that the two populations use the predictors in a similar order; this suggests the transportability limit lies in the *strength*, not the *structure*, of predictor?outcome associations. The clearest exception was food insecurity, which ranked 10th in KCHS but 7th in NHANES pre, consistent with its different measurement (single item vs 10-item module) and much higher prevalence in the US.
142: 
143: Subgroup discrimination in KCHS was fairly uniform (weighted AUROC 0.785?0.859): sex (female 0.822, 95% CI 0.811?0.834; male 0.831, 0.814?0.847), education (0.813?0.830), and age (20?39 years 0.785, 0.764?0.807 < 40?59 years 0.828, 0.811?0.845 < ￣60 years 0.859, 0.847?0.871). In NHANES, discrimination was lower and far less precisely estimated. Pre-pandemic: Mexican American 0.697 (0.631?0.770; n=914), non-Hispanic Black 0.762 (0.729?0.804; n=2,096), other/multiracial 0.772 (0.679?0.868; n=381), other Hispanic 0.777 (0.736?0.817; n=813), non-Hispanic White 0.778 (0.731?0.822; n=2,798), non-Hispanic Asian 0.810 (0.719?0.874; n=886). Post-pandemic: Mexican American 0.689 (0.538?0.805; n=330), non-Hispanic Black 0.727 (0.646?0.788; n=598), non-Hispanic White 0.770 (0.723?0.811; n=3,215), other/multiracial 0.786 (0.701?0.863; n=340), other Hispanic 0.790 (0.751?0.845; n=499), non-Hispanic Asian 0.841 (0.651?0.988; n=228). These intervals are wide and mutually overlapping, and every one of them overlaps the cohort-level interval, so the ordering should not be read as established differential performance; that the lowest point estimate falls in Mexican American respondents in both cycles nonetheless warrants monitoring as an equity caveat (Figure 6). Internal subgroup calibration was good (slope 0.97?1.02 across sex and age strata, 0.93?1.03 including education strata; subgroup CITL ?0.02 to +0.26). In NHANES the subgroup calibration slopes were uniformly below 1 (0.55?0.78, one exception) and subgroup CITL was positive in every group except Mexican American pre-pandemic (up to +1.30), confirming that the external miscalibration is under-prediction of absolute risk and not only over-extreme ranking (＝3.3). Exact subgroup values with CIs are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S9.
144: 
145: On decision-curve analysis (weighted, with cohort-specific screen-all and screen-none default strategies), the model provided positive net benefit exceeding both defaults across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse grid, sensitivity/specificity were 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (NHANES pre), and 0.739/0.667 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.110?0.236) and negative predictive value high (0.948?0.987), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)?a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4?and the 5-item deployment model performed comparably (PPV 0.122 and 0.170; number needed to screen 8.2 and 5.9). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies?screening everyone and screening no one?at threshold probabilities of 0.10?0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4?S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
