===== docs/appendix2_crosswalk.md =====
1: # Multimedia Appendix 2. Year-specific KCHS㏒NHANES harmonization crosswalk and the locked predictor-set amendment
2: 
3: Supports Methods ＝2.5 and ＝2.5.1. Section A documents how each harmonized variable was resolved in every KCHS survey year and in each NHANES cycle. Section B is an annotated English summary of the predictor-set amendment, with contextual notes added where marked; the original Korean document (dated 2026-07-15) is released unchanged with the code and is the authoritative version. The amendment was locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, but after the 14-predictor model had been developed and transferred (see ＝B.5).
4: 
5: ---
6: 
7: ## A. Harmonization crosswalk
8: 
9: ### A.1 Outcome
10: 
11: | Concept | KCHS | NHANES pre (`P_`) | NHANES post (`_L`) | Harmonized definition |
12: |---|---|---|---|---|
13: | Current depression | `mtb_07a1`?`mtb_07i1` (9 items) | `DPQ010`?`DPQ090` | `DPQ010`?`DPQ090` | Nine items scored 0?3, summed; ￣10 = case. KCHS response codes 1?4 recoded to 0?3. Respondents missing any item excluded. |
14: 
15: ### A.2 The 17 harmonized predictors
16: 
17: KCHS variable names are not stable across survey years; the pipeline resolves each concept per year, first by variable name and then by a label regular expression, and writes the resolved name to a crosswalk log (`results/qc_crosswalk.txt`). Predictors 15?17 are the amendment additions. The locked document numbers them food insecurity, employment, oral health; this table reorders them for readability without changing the set.
18: 
19: | # | Concept | KCHS variable(s), by year | NHANES pre | NHANES post | Harmonized definition |
20: |---|---|---|---|---|---|
21: | 1 | Age | `age` (all years) | `RIDAGEYR` | `RIDAGEYR` | Continuous; analytic samples restricted to ￣20 y |
22: | 2 | Sex | `sex` (all years) | `RIAGENDR` | `RIAGENDR` | Male / female |
23: | 3 | Education | `sob_01z1` (all years) | `DMDEDUC2` | `DMDEDUC2` | 4 levels (¯elementary, middle, high, college+) |
24: | 4 | Household income | `fma_24z1` (2017); `fma_14z1` (2018?2025) | `INDFMPIR` | `INDFMPIR` | Within-survey quintiles (rank-based, so the differing instruments are comparable in relative terms) |
25: | 5 | Self-rated health | `qoa_01z1` (all years) | `HUQ010` | `HUQ010` | 5-level ordinal, 1 best ? 5 worst |
26: | 6 | Partnered | `sod_02z2` (2017?2019); `sod_02z3` (2020?2025) | `DMDMARTZ` | `DMDMARTZ` | Married/cohabiting vs not |
27: | 7 | Living alone | `fma_01z1` (2017?2019); `mbhld_co` (2020?2025) | `DMDHHSIZ` (**absent from the pre-pandemic DEMO file**) | `DMDHHSIZ` | Household size = 1. Retained as missing in NHANES pre rather than imputed |
28: | 8 | Smoking | `sma_03z2` (current smoking status) | `SMQ020`, `SMQ040` | `SMQ020`, `SMQ040` | Never / former / current |
29: | 9 | Alcohol | `dra_01z1` (+ `drb_01z3`) | `ALQ111`, `ALQ121` | same | Current drinker (conservative common definition; `ALQ130` was extracted but not used in the harmonized definition) |
30: | 10 | Physical activity | `pha_04z1`/`pha_05z1`/`pha_06z1` (vigorous), `pha_07z1`/`pha_08z1`/`pha_09z1` (moderate), `phb_01z1` (walking); `pha_07z1` not fielded in 2018 (activity derived from the remaining components) | `PAQ650`/`PAQ655`/`PAD660` (vigorous), `PAQ665`/`PAQ670`/`PAD675` (moderate) | `PAD790Q`/`PAD790U`/`PAD800` (moderate), `PAD810Q`/`PAD810U`/`PAD820` (vigorous) | Meets aerobic recommendation (yes/no). Instruments differ between NHANES cycles; the binary recommendation threshold is the conservative common definition |
31: | 11 | Sleep duration | `mtc_01z1` (2017?2019); `mtc_17z1` (2020?2022, 2024, 2025); **not fielded in 2023** | `SLD012` | `SLD012` | Hours per night (continuous) |
32: | 12 | BMI | `oba_bmi` when present (2018, 2020, 2023, 2024); otherwise derived from `oba_02z1` (height) and `oba_03z1` (weight); **neither available in 2019** | `BMXBMI` | `BMXBMI` | Continuous. Self-reported in KCHS, measured in NHANES |
33: | 13 | Hypertension | `hya_04z1` (all years) | `BPQ020` | `BPQ020` | Physician-diagnosed, yes/no |
34: | 14 | Diabetes | `dia_04z1` (all years) | `DIQ010` | `DIQ010` | Physician-diagnosed, yes/no |
35: | **15** | **Employment** | `soa_01z1` (9/9 years) | `OCD150` (P_OCQ) | `OCD150` (OCQ_L) | Binary: KCHS 1 = employed; NHANES 1?2 (working, or has a job but not at work) = employed, 3?4 = not |
36: | **16** | **Self-rated oral health** | `ora_01z1` (9/9 years) | `OHQ845` (P_OHQ) | `OHQ845` (OHQ_L) | 5-level ordinal, 1 best ? 5 worst (same construction as self-rated health) |
37: | **17** | **Food insecurity** | `nue_01z1` (7/9 years; **not fielded 2020?2021**) | `FSDAD` (P_FSQ) | `FSDAD` (FSQ_L) | Binary: KCHS categories 3?4 (sometimes/often insufficient) = insecure; NHANES adult food-security categories 3?4 (low/very low) = insecure. Single item vs 10-item module ? the most weakly harmonized predictor |
38: | ? | Race/ethnicity (fairness analysis only, never a predictor) | not collected | `RIDRETH3` | `RIDRETH3` | NHANES subgroup analysis only |
39: 
40: ### A.3 Complex-survey design variables
41: 
42: | Element | KCHS | NHANES pre | NHANES post |
43: |---|---|---|---|
44: | Weight | `wt` (2017?2018); `wt_p` (2019?2025) | `WTMECPRP` (P_DEMO) | `WTMEC2YR` (DEMO_L) |
45: | Stratum | `kstrata` | `SDMVSTRA` | `SDMVSTRA` |
46: | Primary sampling unit | `jijum_cd` (2017?2018); `spot_no` (2019?2021); `SPOT_NO` (2022?2025) | `SDMVPSU` | `SDMVPSU` |
47: | Design scale | 5,729 strata; 197,117 stratum?PSU combinations | 24 strata; 49 PSUs | 15 strata; 30 PSUs |
48: 
49: ### A.4 Analytic sample derivation
50: 
51: | Cohort | Adults ￣20 y | With complete PHQ-9 | Unweighted prevalence | Weighted prevalence |
52: |---|---|---|---|---|
53: | KCHS 2017?2025 | 2,071,211 | **2,048,510** | 3.607% | 3.58% |
54: | NHANES pre-pandemic | 15,560 (all ages) ⊥ 9,232 adults ￣20 y | **7,888** | 9.229% | 8.45% |
55: | NHANES post-pandemic | 11,933 (all ages) ⊥ 7,809 adults ￣20 y | **5,210** | 12.687% | 12.20% |
56: 
57: Per-year KCHS counts and structural gaps are logged in `results/qc_crosswalk.txt`. Depression-module non-response in NHANES (three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered) was 14.3% pre and 32.7% post and was differential by education and race/ethnicity; see Results ＝3.1 and Limitations ＝4.4.
58: 
59: ### A.5 Variables excluded as circular with the outcome
60: 
61: Pre-specified for exclusion because they re-measure the outcome construct rather than predict it:
62: 
63: | Excluded | Reason |
64: |---|---|
65: | KCHS `mtb_07a1`?`mtb_07i1` | the PHQ-9 items themselves (the outcome) |
66: | KCHS `mtb_01z1` (depressed-mood experience) | a subset of the PHQ-9 construct |
67: | KCHS `mtb_02z1`, `mte_01z1` (mental-health consultation) | consequence/proxy of depression |
68: | KCHS `mtd_01z1`?`mtd_06z1` (suicidal ideation, attempt, counselling) | overlaps PHQ-9 item 9 |
69: | KCHS `qoc_05z1` (EQ-5D anxiety/depression) | directly measures depression |
70: | KCHS `qoc_07z1` (happiness index) | overlapping affective construct |
71: | KCHS `mta_01z1` (perceived stress) | outcome-proximal; also 100% missing in NHANES |
72: | NHANES `HUQ090` (saw a mental-health professional) | consequence/proxy; excluded on the external side as well |
73: 
74: ---
75: 
76: ## B. Predictor-set amendment (English rendition of the locked document)
77: 
78: **Status: written 2026-07-15 and locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted.** The original states that the document is not to be revised after seeing results. It was, however, written after the 14-predictor model had been developed and applied to NHANES: ＝B.5 below reproduces the expectations it records, which reference the then-known internal (0.824) and external (~0.75) performance.
79: 
80: ### B.1 Rationale
81: 
82: The original protocol pre-specified 14 harmonized predictors (protocol Table 1). The supervising investigator approved a re-selection of predictors; the investigators elected to keep the external-validation design unchanged (develop in KCHS, apply to NHANES without refitting) and to expand only the set of variables that both surveys share. Sections ＝2.4 (outcome definition), ＝2.8 (model development) and ＝2.10 (external-validation strategy) of the protocol were therefore left unchanged; only the predictor list was amended.
83: 
84: ### B.2 Eligibility criteria
85: 
86: Candidates were drawn from a crosswalk of all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). A candidate was eligible only if it was (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. These criteria use survey documentation and variable availability only; no NHANES outcome data were consulted.
87: 
88: ### B.3 Predictors added
89: 
90: Employment (`soa_01z1` ㏒ `OCD150`), self-rated oral health (`ora_01z1` ㏒ `OHQ845`), and household food insecurity (`nue_01z1` ㏒ `FSDAD`) were the three concepts adopted. Harmonized definitions are given in Table A.2.
91: 
92: Candidates rejected at this stage, with reasons: additional chronic conditions in KCHS (dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, cataract, arthritis) are fielded in a single year only; stroke and myocardial infarction are recorded in KCHS as *awareness of early warning signs* rather than physician diagnosis, so they do not match the NHANES concept; functional limitation exists only in the NHANES post-pandemic cycle.
93: 
94: ### B.4 Analysis plan (unchanged from the protocol)
95: 
96: Outcome PHQ-9 ￣10 in both surveys, with ￣5 as a sensitivity analysis. Development in KCHS 2017?2025 with three algorithms (elastic net, random forest, LightGBM); the primary model selected on internal cross-validation only, by CV-AUROC, then |calibration slope ? 1|, then parsimony; external performance is never used to choose a model. Preprocessing fitted in the development data and applied unchanged externally. Complex-survey?weighted evaluation with cluster bootstrap CIs. The KCHS-trained model applied to NHANES without refitting, evaluated separately in the pre- and post-pandemic cycles, with intercept/slope recalibration. The 17-predictor and the original 14-predictor models are reported side by side, internally and externally, so that the net effect of the expansion is visible.
97: 
98: ### B.5 Expectations declared in advance
99: 
100: The three additions were expected to be moderate-strength predictors, raising internal AUROC only slightly (roughly 0.824 ⊥ 0.83). **External AUROC of 0.80 was explicitly not expected**, because earlier work had already localized the external gap to Korea?US concept shift rather than to a shortage of predictors. The document states that if the expansion does not improve external performance, that result is to be reported as it stands, since an absence of improvement is itself evidence that the gap is a transportability problem rather than a predictor-count problem.
101: 
102: ### B.6 Deviation log
103: 
104: Deviations from the locked document and the parent protocol are reported here and, rule by rule, in Multimedia Appendix 3 Table S11. They comprise: the post-hoc candidate screening described below; a parsimony margin reported at both the planned 0.02 and a stricter 0.01; an alcohol variable operationalized as current drinking alone rather than current plus at-risk drinking; the planned BMI self-report-versus-measured comparison, prior-diagnosis exclusion and alternative physical-activity definition, none of which the data support; the planned R components, which were not used; and public registration, which was not completed. After the primary analysis was complete, the remaining harmonizable-but-unused items (public-assistance receipt, alcohol frequency and quantity, binge drinking, weekend sleep duration) were screened by comparing the direction of their adjusted associations with depression across the three cohorts. This screening inspected NHANES outcome associations and so lies outside the locked plan; it is reported in Methods ＝2.5.1 as a post-hoc diagnostic. It did not change the predictor set: no item was added, because those with a consistent direction added no external discrimination (汁AUROC <0.001) and the remainder reversed direction between countries.

===== docs/appendix3_utility.md =====
1: # Multimedia Appendix 3. Clinical utility, sample characteristics, and sensitivity analyses
2: 
3: All estimates complex-survey weighted. Supports Results ＝3.1 (participant characteristics and depression-module non-response, Tables S6 and S10), ＝3.6 (parsimonious deployment model, Table S8), ＝3.7 (sensitivity analyses, Table S7), and ＝3.8 (fairness and clinical utility, Tables S1, S4, S5 and S9). The model is positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic instrument.
4: 
5: ## Table S1. Operating characteristics at reference thresholds
6: 
7: The Youden point is computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse threshold grid. Thresholds are selected and evaluated in the same cohort, so these operating characteristics are apparent rather than out-of-sample.
