===== docs/cover_letter.md =====
1: # Cover Letter ? Submission to the Journal of Medical Internet Research (JMIR)
2: 
3: *[Date] ﹞ To the Editor-in-Chief, Journal of Medical Internet Research*
4: 
5: Dear Editor,
6: 
7: We are pleased to submit our Original Paper, **"A Machine Learning Depression Screener Developed in the Korea Community Health Survey and Externally Validated in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey Across the COVID-19 Period: Development and External Validation Study,"** for consideration in JMIR.
8: 
9: **What the study does.** We develop a machine-learning depression screener (PHQ-9 ￣10) in the Korea Community Health Survey (n?2.05 million) and externally validate it?without refitting?in the US NHANES across NCHS-defined pre- and post-COVID-19 cycles. This is, to our knowledge, among the first depression-screening models to be validated across country, health system, and the pandemic distribution shift using an identically defined outcome in both surveys.
10: 
11: **Why it matters for JMIR's readership.** Most published survey-based depression models report internal validation only and, in Korean data, achieve internal AUROCs up to ~0.90 while relying on predictors (e.g., perceived stress, depressed-mood items) that are near-tautological with the outcome. Our study instead asks the question that matters for any deployable digital screener: does it transport? We find that discrimination remains clinically useful but lower on external data (AUROC ~0.77), that calibration drifts but is fully recoverable by lightweight intercept/slope recalibration, and?critically?that this ~0.77 external ceiling withstood every leakage-controlled attempt to raise it, including matching sample size, correcting covariate shift and fitting NHANES directly ? pointing to a limit of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. We establish this rigorously with an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels caps at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reach ~0.83 within Korea) and >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments, and we show that predictor-importance rank order is highly concordant across surveys (Spearman 伏?0.97?0.99). A parsimonious 5- to 7-item version retains most of the external discrimination, supporting a low-burden web/mobile triage tool?squarely within JMIR's scope of internet-based health services.
12: 
13: **Methodological rigor and transparency.** The work follows TRIPOD+AI (2024; checklist enclosed as Multimedia Appendix 1) with PROBAST risk-of-bias self-assessment. We report a predictor-set amendment (14⊥17 predictors) that was documented and locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted ? though after the 14-predictor external result was known, which we state explicitly ? with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; all model fitting, tuning, algorithm selection, and the choice of deployment item count used development-data cross-validation only, and the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search so the comparison is not confounded by tuning effort. We also disclose and correct three silent data-processing defects found in an adversarial data-integrity audit. The analysis code, variable-harmonization dictionary, model card, and protocol/pre-specification documents will be deposited in a public repository on acceptance; generative AI tools assisted with code and drafting under full author verification.
14: 
15: **Declarations.** This manuscript is original, not under consideration elsewhere, and all authors approve submission. We declare no competing interests. The study uses public, de-identified secondary data; raw KCHS microdata are access-controlled and not redistributable, while NHANES data are openly available.
16: 
17: We believe this study will interest JMIR readers working on scalable digital mental-health screening and on the real-world transportability of clinical AI. We suggest reviewers with expertise in clinical prediction modeling, survey epidemiology, and digital mental health, and have no reviewers to exclude.
18: 
19: Thank you for your consideration.
20: 
21: Sincerely,
22: [Corresponding author name, degrees, affiliation, email ? to complete]
23: on behalf of all authors

