27: 
28: ## 3. 반드시 제외 ? 순환/누수 변수 (Circular / leakage)
29: 결과(PHQ-9 ≥10)를 사실상 재측정하는 변수는 **예측변수로 절대 사용하지 않는다.** 성능이 인위적으로 부풀기 때문.
30: 
31: | 제외 변수 | 사유 |
32: |---|---|
33: | `mtb_07a1`~`mtb_07i1` (KCHS PHQ-9 9문항) | **결과변수 그 자체** |
34: | `mtb_01z1` 우울감 경험 여부 | PHQ-9의 부분집합 |
35: | `mtb_02z1`, `mte_01z1` 정신상담 여부 | 우울의 결과/대리지표 |
36: | `mtd_01z1`~`mtd_06z1` 자살생각·시도·상담 | PHQ-9 9번과 중첩 |
37: | `qoc_05z1` EQ-5D 불안우울 | 우울을 직접 측정 |
38: | `qoc_07z1` 행복감지수 | 정동 구성개념 중첩 |
39: | `mta_01z1` 스트레스 인지 | 근접 순환 → **조화모델 제외**(기존 Table 2대로 국내 보조모델 전용) |
40: | NHANES `HUQ090` 정신건강 전문가 상담 | 위와 동일(외부셋에서도 사용 금지) |
41: 
42: ## 4. 분석 계획 (기존 프로토콜 유지 ? 변경 없음)
43: - **결과**: PHQ-9 총점 ≥10 (양국 동일). 민감도: ≥5, 연속점수.
44: - **개발**: KCHS 2017?2025. 알고리즘 3종(elastic net / random forest / LightGBM). **주모델은 내부 CV 기준으로 사전 선택**(CV-AUROC → |CV-OOF 보정slope?1| → 간명성). 외부 성능을 보고 모델을 고르지 않는다.
45: - **전처리**: 개발셋에서만 학습 → 외부에 그대로 적용(누수 금지, §2.6).
46: - **평가**: 복합표본 가중, PSU-군집 부트스트랩 CI (§2.8·2.9).
47: - **외부검증**: KCHS 학습모델을 NHANES에 **재적합 없이** 적용(§2.10). pre/post 분리 + 절편/기울기 재보정.
48: - **비교 기준**: 확장 17변수 모델 vs 기존 14변수 모델을 **내부·외부 모두** 병기 보고한다(확장의 순효과를 정직하게 드러내기 위함).
49: 
50: ## 5. 사전 선언 (Pre-declared expectations)
51: - 신규 3개는 **중간 강도** 예측변수로 예상되며, 내부 AUROC는 소폭 상승(0.824 → 0.83 내외) 예상.
52: - **외부 AUROC 0.80 도달은 기대하지 않는다.** 외부 격차의 원인은 변수 수 부족이 아니라 한·미 간 concept shift로 이미 확인되었다(레버 실험: 결측·가중·알고리즘 조정으로도 외부 ~0.75 불변).
53: - 확장 후 외부가 개선되지 않더라도 **그 결과를 그대로 보고**한다. 개선이 없다는 것 자체가 "격차는 변수가 아니라 전이 문제"라는 근거가 된다.
54: 
55: ## 6. 이력
56: - 2026-07-15: 본 문서 작성·확정. 근거: `results/kchs_full_inventory.csv`(KCHS 616개념), `results/nhanes_var_list_raw.csv`(NHANES 175변수), Codex 전수 대조 결과, `변수목록_KCHS_NHANES.xlsx`.
57: - 선행 정정: 자가건강 NHANES 코드 `HSQ·HSD010` → `HUQ·HUQ010` (파일오류 정정, erratum 기록됨).

===== ..\docs\protocol_text.md =====
1: Development and Cross-National External Validation of a Machine Learning Model for Depression Screening: A Digital Triage Tool Developed in the Korea Community Health Survey and Validated in the US NHANES Across the COVID-19 Period
2: Study Protocol / Research Design
3: Target journal: Journal of Medical Internet Research (JMIR). Alternatives: JMIR Public Health and Surveillance, JMIR Medical Informatics, JMIR Mental Health.
4: Reporting guideline: TRIPOD+AI (2024); risk of bias self-assessed with PROBAST. Protocol pre-registered (e.g., OSF) before analysis.
5: [Heading1] Structured Abstract
6: Background. Depression is a leading contributor to the global burden of disease, and scalable, low-cost screening is needed in community and primary-care settings. Machine learning (ML) prediction models built on national health surveys are common, but most are evaluated only with internal validation. Evidence on whether such models transport to a different country, health system, and population, and whether they remain stable across the distribution shift caused by the COVID-19 pandemic, is scarce.
7: Objective. To develop an ML model for current depression (Patient Health Questionnaire-9 [PHQ-9] ≥10) using the Korea Community Health Survey (KCHS) and to externally validate it in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) across the pre- and post-COVID-19 periods, as the engine for a deployable digital screening and triage tool.
8: Methods. Penalized logistic regression, random forest, and gradient-boosting models will be developed in KCHS using a harmonized predictor set shared with NHANES and evaluated for discrimination, calibration, and clinical utility (decision curve analysis), with SHAP-based interpretation and subgroup fairness. Geographic external validation will be performed in NHANES; temporal validation will contrast the NCHS-defined pre-pandemic (2017?March 2020) and pandemic/post-pandemic (August 2021?August 2023) cycles, with recalibration assessed. A parsimonious model will be derived for digital deployment.
9: Results. This is a protocol; results are pending. Planned tables and figures, with pre-specified metrics and placeholders, are provided.
10: Conclusions. The study will quantify the cross-national and cross-pandemic transportability of a survey-based depression screening model and assess its suitability as a low-burden digital triage tool.
11: Keywords: depression; PHQ-9; machine learning; prediction model; external validation; transportability; COVID-19; digital health screening; NHANES; Korea Community Health Survey.
12: 
13: [Heading1] 1. Introduction
14: Depression is among the leading causes of years lived with disability worldwide, and its detection at the community and primary-care level remains incomplete. Brief self-report instruments such as the PHQ-9 enable population-scale screening, yet applying a fixed cut-off uniformly across heterogeneous populations does not prioritize those at highest risk. Risk-stratification tools that combine routinely available sociodemographic and behavioral information could support more efficient triage, and, when delivered through web or mobile interfaces, could scale at low marginal cost?an application squarely within the scope of internet-based health services.
15: Machine learning models for depression have been developed repeatedly from large national surveys, including the KCHS and the Korea National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey, typically reporting strong discrimination. However, most published models are assessed only through internal validation (cross-validation or a hold-out split from the same survey). Internal performance is an optimistic indicator of real-world behavior: it does not establish that a model transports to a different sampling frame, health system, or population, and it does not test stability under temporal distribution shift.
16: The COVID-19 pandemic constitutes a natural distribution shift for mental-health prediction. Population distributions of depressive symptoms and of their behavioral correlates (physical activity, sleep, social contact, employment) changed during the pandemic, and a model trained before 2020 may show degraded calibration afterward even if discrimination is preserved. Whether recalibration?updating only the intercept and slope without re-estimating predictor effects?restores clinical usefulness is a practically important, under-studied question for deployed screening tools.
17: This protocol addresses these gaps with a development-and-external-validation design that is unusually well suited to the outcome of interest. Depression is operationalized identically in both data sources: the KCHS and NHANES each administer the nine-item PHQ-9 with 0?3 scoring, so the outcome definition (sum ≥10) is common across surveys, minimizing outcome harmonization error. The two surveys differ in country, health system, racial/ethnic composition, and survey mode (household interview vs mobile examination center), making NHANES a substantively external validation set rather than a near-replicate. NHANES additionally provides National Center for Health Statistics-defined pre-pandemic (2017?March 2020) and pandemic/post-pandemic (August 2021?August 2023) data, enabling temporal validation within the same external population.
18: We therefore pursue four aims: (1) develop an ML depression-screening model in the KCHS; (2) externally validate it in the US NHANES (geographic transportability); (3) validate it separately in the pre- and post-COVID-19 NHANES cycles and quantify the effect of recalibration (temporal transportability); and (4) derive a parsimonious model suitable for a low-burden digital self-screening and triage tool. We hypothesize that the full model will achieve an AUROC of at least 0.80 internally; that discrimination will remain clinically useful on external validation while calibration may deteriorate; that recalibration will significantly improve calibration; and that the parsimonious model will incur only a small, clinically negligible loss of performance.
19: 
20: [Heading1] 2. Methods
21: [Heading2] 2.1 Study design and reporting standards
22: This is a retrospective secondary-data study developing a clinical prediction model with external validation. Development and validation are reported in accordance with TRIPOD+AI (2024); risk of bias is self-assessed with PROBAST. The protocol is registered before analysis, and all preprocessing code, hyperparameter grids, and a model card are released in a public repository, consistent with open-science reporting.
23: [Heading2] 2.2 Data sources
24: Development ? Korea Community Health Survey (KCHS). A nationwide survey by the Korea Disease Control and Prevention Agency (KDCA) of community-dwelling adults aged ≥19 years, conducted annually through in-person household interviews (~230,000 respondents/year) using a stratified, clustered, weighted complex-sample design. Only survey years in which the PHQ-9 is administered will be used; the exact PHQ-9 collection years must be confirmed from the year-specific raw-data user guide (원시자료 이용지침서, Part 4: Variable Descriptions) before finalizing the development sample.
25: External validation ? US NHANES. A nationally representative survey by the US National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS) combining household interview and mobile examination center assessment, with a stratified, clustered, weighted design. Depression is measured by the DPQ (PHQ-9) module (adults analyzed). Two periods are used as separate validation sets following the NCHS definition: pre-pandemic (2017?March 2020, the ‘P_’ combined files) and pandemic/post-pandemic (August 2021?August 2023, the ‘_L’ files).
26: [Heading2] 2.3 Participants and eligibility
27: [ListParagraph] Included: adults meeting a common lower age bound harmonized across surveys, with all nine PHQ-9 items answered.
28: [ListParagraph] Excluded: PHQ-9 items incomplete beyond the analytic threshold. Pregnant respondents and those with a prior physician diagnosis of depression are retained in the main analysis and examined in sensitivity analyses.
29: [Heading2] 2.4 Outcome
30: The primary outcome is current depression, defined as a PHQ-9 total score ≥10, computed identically in both surveys (nine items, 0?3 each). Sensitivity analyses use a ≥5 cut-off and the continuous score.
31: [Heading2] 2.5 Candidate predictors and variable harmonization
32: Only variables that can be defined identically in both surveys?the intersection of the two instruments?enter the harmonized model used for external validation. Table 1 gives the concept-level mapping with NHANES file and variable codes and the harmonized modeling definition. NHANES variable codes are exact; KCHS entries are specified at the questionnaire-item / published-indicator level and the raw variable code must be confirmed in the year-specific user guide (variable names vary by year).
