223: [Heading2] 3.4 Interpretability and fairness
224: Global SHAP importance and top-predictor rank concordance between surveys are reported (Figure 4). Subgroup discrimination/calibration/error rates by sex, age, education, income, and race/ethnicity are tabulated to characterize equity of performance.
225: [Heading2] 3.5 Parsimonious deployment model
226: Performance of the 5?10-item model versus the full model is reported to quantify the accuracy?burden trade-off relevant to a digital self-screening form.
227: 
228: [Heading1] 4. Discussion
229: [Heading2] 4.1 Principal findings (anticipated)
230: This study will provide, to our knowledge, one of the first evaluations of a depression-screening ML model that is developed in one country’s national survey and externally validated in another country’s survey using an identically defined PHQ-9 outcome, while simultaneously testing stability across the COVID-19 distribution shift. The design directly targets the transportability question that internal validation cannot answer, and it clarifies whether lightweight recalibration is sufficient to maintain clinical usefulness after a population-level shock.
231: [Heading2] 4.2 Comparison with prior work
232: Prior KCHS- and KNHANES-based ML depression models generally report internal validation only; cross-national external validation and explicit pre/post-COVID temporal validation are rare. By harmonizing predictors to the intersection of the two surveys and using an identical outcome, this protocol reduces the outcome- and label-shift confounding that complicates cross-dataset comparisons.
233: [Heading2] 4.3 Strengths
234: [ListParagraph] identical PHQ-9 outcome across development and validation, minimizing outcome harmonization error.
235: [ListParagraph] genuinely external validation across country, health system, race/ethnicity, and survey mode.
236: [ListParagraph] temporal validation across NCHS-defined pre/post-COVID periods with recalibration analysis.
237: [ListParagraph] interpretability, fairness, clinical-utility (DCA), and a deployment-oriented parsimonious model, aligned with digital-health translation.
238: [Heading2] 4.4 Limitations
239: [ListParagraph] cross-sectional data: the model screens for current symptoms rather than predicting future onset.
240: [ListParagraph] self-report and recall bias; differing measurement of alcohol, physical activity, income, and BMI across surveys.
241: [ListParagraph] residual population differences (race/ethnicity, health system) may limit transportability.
242: [ListParagraph] dependence on the KCHS years in which PHQ-9 is collected, which constrains development-sample size and calendar coverage.
243: [Heading2] 4.5 Conclusions
244: By quantifying cross-national and cross-pandemic transportability and by delivering a parsimonious, interpretable, and calibration-updatable model, this study will indicate whether a survey-derived depression screener can serve as a scalable digital triage tool and how it should be maintained when populations shift.
245: 
246: [Heading1] Appendix A. Pre-analysis confirmations
247: [ListParagraph] Confirm the exact KCHS survey years in which PHQ-9 is administered (year-specific user guide, Part 4).
248: [ListParagraph] Confirm year-specific KCHS raw variable codes for every Table 1 item.
249: [ListParagraph] Compute depression event counts per NHANES period to confirm calibration/DCA precision.
250: [ListParagraph] Finalize the common operational definitions for alcohol, physical activity, and income.
251: [ListParagraph] Confirm NHANES cycle-specific variable names (e.g., DMDMARTZ vs DMDMARTL; ALQ items).
252: [Heading1] Appendix B. Key references (verify formatting to JMIR style)
253: Kroenke K, Spitzer RL, Williams JBW. The PHQ-9: validity of a brief depression severity measure. J Gen Intern Med. 2001;16(9):606?613.
254: Collins GS, Moons KGM, Dhiman P, et al. TRIPOD+AI statement: updated guidance for reporting clinical prediction models that use regression or machine learning methods. BMJ. 2024;385:e078378.
255: Moons KGM, Wolff RF, Riley RD, et al. PROBAST: assessing the risk of bias and applicability of prediction model studies. Ann Intern Med. 2019;170(1):W1?W33.
256: Vickers AJ, Elkin EB. Decision curve analysis: a novel method for evaluating prediction models. Med Decis Making. 2006;26(6):565?574.
257: Lundberg SM, Lee S-I. A unified approach to interpreting model predictions (SHAP). Adv Neural Inf Process Syst. 2017;30.
258: National Center for Health Statistics. NHANES Mental Health ? Depression Screener (DPQ) documentation. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention.
259: Korea Disease Control and Prevention Agency. Korea Community Health Survey raw-data user guide (지역사회건강조사 원시자료 이용지침서).
260: 
261: [Heading1] Appendix C. Data availability and access
262: Both datasets are de-identified and free of charge, but their access procedures differ: KCHS microdata are released only after an application and review, whereas NHANES data are openly downloadable. Raw KCHS microdata may not be redistributed; only approved users may obtain them.
263: [Heading2] C.1 Korea Community Health Survey (KCHS)
264: Provider / portal. Korea Disease Control and Prevention Agency (KDCA). Portal: https://chs.kdca.go.kr (지역사회건강조사). Microdata are provided under the KDCA regulation on raw-data release (질병관리청 예규 제124호) through a request-and-review process, not open download.
265: Procedure:
266: [ListParagraph] Create an account on the portal and agree to the data-use pledge (이용서약).
267: [ListParagraph] Submit a raw-data request (원시자료 자료요청) specifying the survey years, target variables, and research purpose.
268: [ListParagraph] KDCA reviews the request (심의; typically ~2?3 business days).
269: [ListParagraph] On approval, download the microdata (download link valid for 1 year from the request date).
270: Materials provided. Raw microdata plus the raw-data user guide (원시자료 이용지침서, including Part 4 variable descriptions) and the questionnaires (조사표) ? these are needed to confirm year-specific variable codes and the exact years in which the PHQ-9 was administered. A separate open statistics/indicator viewer exists on the portal, but analytic microdata require the request above.
271: [Heading2] C.2 US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES)
272: Provider / portal. US National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS), Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Portal: https://wwwn.cdc.gov/nchs/nhanes/. Data are open access with no application required.
273: Procedure:
274: [ListParagraph] Browse data by cycle and component: Demographics, Questionnaire, Examination, and Laboratory; each file has an online documentation/codebook.
275: [ListParagraph] Download the SAS transport (.XPT) files directly. For this study: pre-pandemic 2017?March 2020 uses the ‘P_’ prefix (e.g., P_DEMO, P_DPQ, P_SMQ, P_ALQ, P_PAQ, P_SLQ, P_BMX, P_HSQ, P_BPQ, P_DIQ); August 2021?August 2023 uses the ‘_L’ suffix (e.g., DEMO_L, DPQ_L, SMQ_L, ALQ_L, PAQ_L, SLQ_L, BMX_L, HSQ_L, BPQ_L, DIQ_L).
276: [ListParagraph] Merge components within a cycle by the respondent sequence number (SEQN), then apply the survey design variables (strata, PSU) and the appropriate MEC/interview weights following the NHANES Analytic Guidelines.
277: [Heading2] C.3 Reproducibility note
278: Because KCHS microdata are access-controlled and non-redistributable, we will release the analysis code and a variable-harmonization dictionary (not the raw KCHS data) so that approved users can reproduce the pipeline; NHANES files are openly available and can be re-obtained directly from NCHS. Exact URLs and access dates will be recorded in the final Data Availability statement.
279: 
280: ===== STATS =====
