118: [Heading2] 2.6 Missing data
119: Multiple imputation (or tree-native handling) is applied within the development data; the imputation and preprocessing pipeline learned in development is applied unchanged to each external set to prevent leakage. External-set information is never used to refit preprocessing.
120: [Heading2] 2.7 Sample size
121: Development power is not limiting given the KCHS size; the constraints are overfitting control and calibration stability. The number of depression events per NHANES cycle governs the precision of calibration and decision-curve estimates and is computed a priori to confirm adequacy for external evaluation.
122: [Heading2] 2.8 Model development
123: [ListParagraph] Algorithms: penalized logistic regression (elastic net; interpretable reference), random forest, and gradient boosting (XGBoost/LightGBM); optionally a shallow neural network.
124: [ListParagraph] Tuning: repeated stratified k-fold cross-validation for tuning; a separate hold-out for internal performance.
125: [ListParagraph] Imbalance: class weighting / focal loss / (if needed) SMOTE applied within training folds only; the operating threshold is chosen after calibration on clinical criteria.
126: [ListParagraph] Weights: the treatment of complex-survey weights in ML (weighted training vs unweighted training with weighted evaluation) is pre-specified and probed in sensitivity analyses.
127: [Heading2] 2.9 Performance evaluation
128: [ListParagraph] Discrimination: AUROC and AUPRC with 95% CIs.
129: [ListParagraph] Calibration: calibration plot, calibration slope and intercept, Brier score, expected calibration error?the central external-validation metrics.
130: [ListParagraph] Clinical utility: decision curve analysis (net benefit) across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range.
131: [ListParagraph] Operating characteristics: sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV at the selected threshold.
132: [Heading2] 2.10 External validation strategy
133: [ListParagraph] Geographic: the KCHS-trained model is applied to NHANES without refitting.
134: [ListParagraph] Temporal/COVID: evaluation is repeated separately in NHANES pre (2017?March 2020) and post (August 2021?August 2023).
135: [ListParagraph] Recalibration: if calibration degrades, intercept/slope recalibration (or Platt/temperature scaling) is applied and the recovery quantified?updating calibration only, without re-estimating predictor effects (a deployment-friendly update).
136: [Heading2] 2.11 Interpretability, fairness, and deployment
137: SHAP values summarize global and local predictor contributions, and the rank concordance of top predictors between KCHS and NHANES is examined as a mechanistic view of transportability. Fairness is assessed by reporting AUROC, calibration, and error rates across sex, age, education, income, and (in NHANES) race/ethnicity. For deployment, a parsimonious model using the top 5?10 predictors is derived to minimize item burden in a self-administered web/mobile form; a conceptual triage workflow routes low-risk users to self-management resources and higher-risk users to professional assessment. The tool is positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a standalone diagnostic or clinical-decision system.
138: [Heading2] 2.12 Sensitivity analyses and software
139: Sensitivity analyses cover the outcome cut-off (กร5/กร10), weighting approach, self-reported vs measured BMI, exclusion of prior-diagnosis respondents, the harmonized age bound, and alternative imputation. Analyses use Python (scikit-learn, xgboost, lightgbm, shap) and R (rms, dcurves, survey); code is released publicly.
140: [Heading2] 2.13 Ethics
141: Both KCHS and NHANES are public, de-identified secondary datasets. Institutional review board exemption or approval is obtained per local policy; data-use terms are followed and no re-identification is attempted.
